# Transport Layer
What is the Transport Layer?
The Transport Layer is the third layer of the TCP/IP Model and the fourth layer of the OSI Model.

It is responsible for providing end-to-end communication between applications running on different devices.

In simple words, the Transport Layer ensures that data sent from one application reaches the correct application on another device.

Example
When you send a message on WhatsApp, the Transport Layer ensures that the message reaches the recipient's WhatsApp application correctly.


# Why is the Transport Layer Used?

The Transport Layer is used to ensure reliable and efficient communication between devices.

Main Reasons
To deliver data from source to destination.
To ensure reliable communication.
To detect and recover from errors.
To control the flow of data.
To identify applications using port numbers.
Example

While downloading a file, the Transport Layer makes sure that every part of the file is received correctly.

# Where is the Transport Layer Used?
The Transport Layer is used whenever applications communicate over a network.

Used In
Web Browsing
Email
Video Streaming
Online Gaming
Video Calls
File Transfers
Example

When you open YouTube, the Transport Layer manages communication between your browser and the YouTube server.

# How Does the Transport Layer Work?
Step 1: Receive Data
The Application Layer sends data to the Transport Layer.

Example
A browser sends a request to open Google.

Step 2: Segmentation
Large data is divided into smaller pieces called segments.

Example
A 100 MB file is broken into many smaller segments before transmission.

Step 3: Add Port Numbers
The Transport Layer adds source and destination port numbers.

This helps identify which application should receive the data.

Example
HTTP → Port 80
HTTPS → Port 443
FTP → Port 21
SSH → Port 22

Step 4: Data Transmission
The segments are sent to the Internet Layer for routing.

Step 5: Reassembly
At the destination, all segments are reassembled in the correct order.

Example
The downloaded file is reconstructed exactly as it was sent.


# Protocols of the Transport Layer
There are two main protocols:

1. TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)
TCP is a connection-oriented and reliable protocol.

Features:
Reliable communication
Error checking
Acknowledgements
Retransmission of lost data
Ordered delivery

Example
When downloading a PDF file:

If segment 5 is lost,
TCP requests segment 5 again before completing the download.

> Uses
Web Browsing
Online Banking
Emails
File Transfers

> Advantage
Data arrives correctly and completely.

>Disadvantage
Slightly slower because of error checking.

2. UDP (User Datagram Protocol)
UDP is a connectionless protocol.
It focuses on speed rather than reliability.

Features
Fast communication
No acknowledgements
No retransmission
No guaranteed delivery

Example
While watching a live cricket match:

If one video packet is lost, UDP does not resend it because speed is more important.

Uses
Video Streaming
Online Gaming
Video Calls
Live Broadcasts

Advantage
Very fast.

Disadvantage
Some data may be lost.

> Important Functions of the Transport Layer
1. Segmentation
Breaks large data into smaller segments.
Example
A 1 GB file is divided into many segments.

2. Flow Control
Controls the speed of data transmission.
Example
If the receiver is slow, the sender reduces its transmission speed.

3. Error Control
Detects transmission errors.
Example
If a segment is corrupted, TCP requests it again.

4. Multiplexing
Allows multiple applications to use the network at the same time.
Example

You can:
Watch YouTube
Browse Google
Download a file

all simultaneously on the same device.

5. Port Addressing
Uses port numbers to identify applications.
Example
A web request goes to Port 443 (HTTPS), while an email may use a different port.

> Real-Life Example
Imagine you are sending a 500-page book to a friend.

> Application Layer
Creates the book.

> Transport Layer
Divides the book into chapters, numbers them, checks for missing chapters, and ensures they arrive in order.

> Internet Layer
Chooses the route.

 > Network Access Layer
Physically delivers the package.

This is exactly how the Transport Layer works with data.







Interview Questions
Q1. What is the main purpose of the Transport Layer?

Answer: To provide end-to-end communication and reliable data delivery between applications.

Q2. Which protocols work at the Transport Layer?

Answer: TCP and UDP.

Q3. Which protocol is reliable?

Answer: TCP.

Q4. Which protocol is faster?

Answer: UDP.

Q5. What is a segment?

Answer: A small piece of data created by the Transport Layer for transmission.